1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:03,530
Forty meters of air, then the ocean.

2
00:00:04,030 --> 00:00:06,780
That is the drop from a cruise

3
00:00:06,830 --> 00:00:09,430
ship's top deck at twenty knots.

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:12,690
Around two dozen people go over the

5
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:15,290
side of cruise ships every single year,

6
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:17,570
and only one in five of them

7
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:19,220
ever comes home alive.

8
00:00:20,183 --> 00:00:23,493
The fall lasts under three seconds and

9
00:00:23,543 --> 00:00:25,773
you hit the water near ninety kilometers

10
00:00:25,823 --> 00:00:29,643
an hour, so go in feet first, arms crossed.

11
00:00:30,064 --> 00:00:33,494
Then the real danger begins, because cold

12
00:00:33,544 --> 00:00:36,554
water forces an involuntary gasp, and if

13
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:39,504
your face is under, that gasp is seawater.

14
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:43,140
Survival experts have a rule for this,

15
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:46,180
and its first number is sixty seconds

16
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:48,990
spent doing nothing but slowing your breath.

17
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:53,120
A hundred thousand tons at twenty knots

18
00:00:53,170 --> 00:00:56,020
cannot simply stop, it covers ten meters

19
00:00:56,070 --> 00:00:59,150
every second and needs a kilometer to halt.

20
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:03,640
If someone saw you fall, a lifebuoy

21
00:01:03,690 --> 00:01:06,300
trailing light and smoke marks your spot

22
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:09,490
in the water while the captain loops the ship back.

23
00:01:10,517 --> 00:01:13,127
That turn takes fifteen minutes, and the

24
00:01:13,177 --> 00:01:16,007
rule's second number, ten minutes, is how

25
00:01:16,057 --> 00:01:18,897
long your arms keep working in cold water.

26
00:01:20,029 --> 00:01:24,519
Temperature decides everything, warm Caribbean water buys

27
00:01:24,569 --> 00:01:29,549
most of a day, ten degree water buys one hour.

28
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:33,970
Instinct says swim, but swimming drains heat

29
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:36,930
far faster, so hug your knees to

30
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:39,340
your chest and let the ring hold you.

31
00:01:40,209 --> 00:01:43,859
Here is the twist, cruise railings stand

32
00:01:43,909 --> 00:01:46,099
over a meter tall by law, so

33
00:01:46,149 --> 00:01:48,889
nobody slips, people climb them for a photo.

34
00:01:50,488 --> 00:01:52,908
So float, never swim, and remember this

35
00:01:53,488 --> 00:01:55,418
began on the wrong side of a

36
00:01:55,468 --> 00:01:58,908
railing, forty meters of air, one mistake.
